      ],
      "Duty Description": "The UX Research duty is the team's link to real users. Instead of guessing what people need, researchers plan and run studies, interviews, usability tests, and surveys, then turn what they hear into clear findings the whole team can act on. It is ongoing work; strong teams keep discovering all the way through a project rather than researching once and moving on.",
      "What Does It Take for This Duty To Succeed?": "Succeeding here takes real research skills like interviewing, experiment design, and spotting patterns in messy data, along with the ability to tell a clear story with what you find. Just as important are the team habits: listening well, staying curious, and building enough trust that people share honest feedback.",
      "Why Is This Duty Important to Project Success?": "This duty keeps the team honest about what users actually need, so effort goes toward real problems instead of assumptions. Its research plans and reports feed decisions at every milestone, and by surfacing issues early it saves the team from building the wrong thing.",
      "You Might Enjoy This Duty If...": "You might enjoy this duty if you are curious about people, like listening more than talking, and get a kick out of turning messy human behavior into clear insight the team can use.",
      "Where Can I Learn More?": "Nielsen Norman Group, a trusted source for UX research and design: https://www.nngroup.com/"
    },
