        },
        {
          "id": "spf:competitive-analysis",
          "label": "Competitive Analysis"
        },
        {
          "id": "spf:prioritized-backlog-tasks",
          "label": "Prioritized Backlog Tasks"
        },
        {
          "id": "spf:roadmap",
          "label": "Roadmap"
        },
        {
          "id": "spf:refined-backlog",
          "label": "Refined Backlog"
        },
        {
          "id": "spf:sprint-plan",
          "label": "Sprint Plan"
        },
        {
          "id": "spf:sprint-goals",
          "label": "Sprint Goals"
        }
      ],
      "Duty Description": "The Product Ownership duty decides what the team should build next and in what order. Owners maintain the backlog, prioritize the work, and keep a roadmap so the team always has a clear, ranked list to pull from. Priorities shift as the team learns, so this is constant, iterative work rather than a plan set once at the start.",
      "What Does It Take for This Duty To Succeed?": "It takes sharp prioritization, backlog and epic management, and the judgment to say this now, that later, and explain why. Add strong stakeholder skills, since much of the job is balancing many voices and building buy-in for the plan.",
      "Why Is This Duty Important to Project Success?": "This duty keeps the team pointed at the most valuable work instead of doing a little of everything. By owning the backlog and sprint goals, it turns a big pile of ideas into a focused plan, which is what lets an agile team ship steadily.",
      "You Might Enjoy This Duty If...": "You might enjoy this duty if you like making decisions, can weigh many priorities at once, and enjoy owning the details of getting the right thing delivered.",
      "Where Can I Learn More?": "Atlassian's guide to product management: https://www.atlassian.com/agile/product-management"
    },
